
# Build a Complete Community Connect Website

Build a complete, modern, professional, responsive, and functional website called **Community Connect — Community Volunteer Coordination and Resource Sharing Platform**.

The website should help people volunteer, donate surplus food, share useful items, request community assistance, and connect with voluntary blood donors.

**Main tagline:** “Small Actions, Big Impact.”

**Hero heading:** “Together, We Can Make a Difference.”

**Important:** Build the complete working website, not just a UI design or static landing page. All buttons, navigation links, forms, filters, dashboards, and interactive features must work. Use the following requirements as the complete project specification.

## 1. Website Design and Colour Theme

Use a consistent **Teal + Warm Yellow + Charcoal** colour theme throughout the entire website.

- Primary Teal: `#0D9488`
- Warm Yellow: `#FDE047`
- Warm White: `#FFFEF7`
- Charcoal: `#27272A`
- Soft Mint: `#CCFBF1`
- White: `#FFFFFF`

### Apply the colours consistently

- Navigation bar: teal background, white text, and clear active navigation indicators.
- Hero section: teal gradient, white heading, readable text, and a warm-yellow call-to-action button.
- Page backgrounds: warm white and white sections.
- Service cards: white background, teal icons, rounded corners, subtle shadows, and soft mint highlights.
- Primary buttons: teal with white text.
- Important call-to-action buttons: warm yellow with charcoal text.
- Body text: charcoal for readability.
- Dashboard: teal and soft mint cards with warm-yellow highlights.
- Kindness points and achievements: warm-yellow accents.
- Forms and input fields: white backgrounds, clear borders, visible focus states, and teal highlights.
- Footer: charcoal background with white text and teal/yellow accents.
- Smart Help Matcher: teal headings, soft mint background, and attractive result cards.

Use modern typography, consistent spacing, smooth scrolling, subtle animations, accessible contrast, attractive icons, and responsive layouts. The website must look excellent on mobile phones, tablets, laptops, and desktops.

## 2. Navigation Bar

Create a responsive navigation bar with:

- Home
- Volunteer Connect
- FoodShare
- ShareCare
- HelpNear
- Blood Donor Connect
- Smart Help Matcher
- Impact Dashboard
- About Us
- Contact

Include a prominent **Get Involved** button.

On mobile devices, use a working hamburger menu. All navigation links must lead to the correct section or page.

## 3. Homepage

Create an engaging homepage with:

- The Community Connect logo or a simple original logo design.
- The heading “Together, We Can Make a Difference.”
- The tagline “Small Actions, Big Impact.”
- A short introduction explaining how the platform connects people who need help with people willing to help.
- A primary “Get Involved” button.
- A secondary “Find Help” button.
- Attractive illustrations or icons representing volunteers, food donations, item sharing, community support, and blood donor connections.
- A section showing the five main services.
- A “How It Works” section explaining how people can participate.
- A community impact section with statistics.
- A testimonial-style section using clearly labelled sample/demo content.
- A footer with navigation, contact information fields, and useful links.

Any sample impact statistics must be clearly identified as demo data until real activity is recorded.

## 4. Volunteer Connect Module

Build a functional volunteer registration and event coordination module.

Features:

- Volunteer registration form with name, email, optional phone number, interests, skills, and general availability.
- Volunteer opportunity listings with title, description, date, location or meeting point, required volunteers, and status.
- Search and filter opportunities by category, date, and availability.
- Register for an opportunity.
- Display registration confirmation and current registration status.
- Allow users to view their registered opportunities.
- Track volunteer hours when hours are recorded or approved.
- Show completed and upcoming volunteer activities.
- Include a volunteer appreciation section with certificates or badges as demo features if certificate generation is not implemented.

Validate forms and display helpful error messages.

## 5. FoodShare Module

Build a food donation and collection management module.

Features:

- A form for listing surplus food donations.
- Fields for food description, food category, quantity, preparation or packaging time where relevant, pickup window, general pickup location, and donor contact preference.
- A list of available food donations.
- Search and filter donations by category and availability.
- Allow an interested recipient or volunteer to request a collection.
- Show statuses such as Available, Collection Requested, Collected, and Completed.
- Provide a way to update the status of a donation.
- Display confirmation messages after successful actions.
- Show a donation history or tracking section.

Include responsible food-sharing guidance. Do not label food as safe automatically; users must follow appropriate food-handling and local food-safety requirements.

## 6. ShareCare Module

Build an item donation and resource-sharing module for books, clothes, stationery, educational materials, and other useful essentials.

Features:

- Add a donation with item name, category, description, quantity, condition, and general pickup location.
- Display available donated items in attractive cards.
- Search and filter items by category and condition.
- Allow people to request an item.
- Show request status, including Available, Requested, Reserved, and Donated.
- Allow the donor or authorized administrator to update item status.
- Display donation history and successful exchanges.
- Show a confirmation message after a request or status update.

Do not expose private home addresses publicly.

## 7. HelpNear Module

Build a community assistance module for non-emergency needs.

Features:

- A form for submitting a request for assistance.
- Categories such as grocery assistance, help with errands, educational support, technology assistance, and support for elderly community members.
- Fields for a short description, urgency level, general location, preferred assistance time, and contact preference.
- Display assistance requests in a list or card layout.
- Allow suitable volunteers to offer help or accept an assigned task.
- Display request statuses such as Open, Volunteer Assigned, In Progress, and Completed.
- Allow authorized users to update the task status.
- Provide a simple task history.
- Include clear confirmation and validation messages.

Do not publish sensitive personal information. Clearly state that HelpNear is for non-emergency assistance and direct users to appropriate local emergency services in emergencies.

## 8. Blood Donor Connect Module

Create a privacy-conscious module for voluntary blood donor connections.

Features:

- A voluntary donor interest registration form.
- Fields for name or display name, preferred contact method, city or general area, and blood group if the person chooses to provide it.
- A directory of participating donors using only the information they have consented to share.
- Search and filter by blood group and general location.
- A way to express interest in contacting a donor or submit a connection request.
- A clear consent and privacy notice.
- An option to withdraw participation or remove a demo donor profile.
- Clear notices that blood donation eligibility and medical suitability must be confirmed by qualified healthcare professionals.

Never display private phone numbers, email addresses, or precise addresses publicly. Do not claim that a donor is medically eligible based only on their profile.

## 9. Smart Help Matcher

Build an interactive **Smart Help Matcher** that guides users to the most suitable service.

Display this heading:

**“Find the Right Way to Help.”**

Ask users what they want to do using a dropdown or attractive selection cards.

Options:

1. I want to volunteer.
2. I want to donate surplus food.
3. I want to donate books, clothes, or other items.
4. I need community assistance.
5. I want to connect with blood donors.

When a user selects an option and clicks **Find My Match**, show the correct recommendation.

- Volunteer → Volunteer Connect
- Food donation → FoodShare
- Item donation → ShareCare
- Community assistance → HelpNear
- Blood donor connection → Blood Donor Connect

Each result must include a short explanation, a relevant icon, and a working button that navigates to the corresponding module.

Requirements:

- Show a helpful message if no option has been selected.
- Update the result immediately after a valid selection and button click.
- Ensure every recommendation links to the correct section.
- Use accessible labels and keyboard-friendly controls.
- Implement this as a transparent, rule-based matching feature. Do not falsely claim it uses artificial intelligence or machine learning.

## 10. Kindness Points and Achievements

Create a simple gamification system to encourage community participation.

Features:

- Award demo points for completed volunteer activities, completed donations, and verified assistance tasks.
- Display the user's points and achievement badges.
- Include badges such as First Contribution, Food Saver, Community Helper, and Volunteer Champion.
- Display progress toward the next achievement.
- Include a clear explanation of how points are earned.

Only award real points when the relevant activity is recorded or confirmed. Clearly identify sample accounts and sample scores as demo data.

Do not allow users to award themselves unlimited points simply by clicking a button repeatedly.

## 11. QR Donation Confirmation

Create a QR-based donation confirmation feature.

Features:

- Provide a QR code or QR-based confirmation workflow for a donation or collection.
- Associate the confirmation with a unique donation or transaction reference.
- After a valid confirmation, update the donation or collection status.
- Prevent the same transaction from being confirmed repeatedly.
- Show a clear success or failure message.

If secure QR verification requires a backend, implement a clearly labelled demo workflow first and document what is needed for production. Do not pretend that a visual QR code alone verifies a real transaction.

## 12. Impact Dashboard

Build a visually attractive dashboard displaying community activity.

Include cards for:

- Total volunteer registrations.
- Completed volunteer activities.
- Volunteer hours recorded.
- Food donations collected.
- Items donated.
- Assistance requests completed.
- Participating donors, where consent has been provided.
- Community contributions recorded.

Include attractive charts or progress visualizations showing activity by category and over time.

Requirements:

- Use actual application records when available.
- Clearly label any sample figures as demo data.
- Update the dashboard when relevant records change.
- Do not show fabricated statistics as real community impact.
- Make the dashboard responsive and easy to understand.

## 13. Elderly-Friendly Accessibility Mode

Add an optional accessibility mode for users who prefer a simpler interface.

Features:

- Larger text.
- Larger buttons and touch targets.
- Clearer spacing.
- Simple language.
- High-contrast controls.
- Reduced motion where appropriate.
- Easy-to-find assistance options.

Provide a clearly visible toggle to enable and disable the mode. Make the controls work on desktop and mobile.

## 14. User Accounts and Data Management

Provide a sensible user experience for donors, volunteers, recipients, and administrators.

Where supported, include:

- Registration and login.
- Basic user profile management.
- My Activities or My Requests page.
- A view of submitted donations, registrations, requests, and their statuses.
- An administrative interface for managing listings, assigning tasks, and moderating records.
- Input validation and clear error messages.
- Confirmation messages for important actions.

Use a proper backend and database if available. If the project is initially frontend-only, create a functional local demo with persistent browser storage where appropriate, and clearly explain its limitations.

Do not store passwords in plain text or expose secret keys in frontend code. Use secure authentication and authorization for any production backend. Protect personal information and restrict administrative actions to authorized administrators.

## 15. Contact and About Sections

Create an About Us section explaining the purpose of Community Connect, its five services, and how community members can participate.

Create a Contact section with a working, validated contact form containing name, email, subject, and message.

Display a success message only when the message has actually been accepted by the implemented form handler or backend. If no email service is configured, clearly label the contact form as a demo and explain how to connect a real service.

Do not invent official phone numbers, email addresses, organization names, or testimonials.

## 16. Technical Requirements

- Build a clean, maintainable, responsive website.
- Use a consistent component and styling system.
- Keep the design consistent across all modules.
- Use semantic HTML, accessible form labels, keyboard navigation, and visible focus states.
- Ensure buttons and links perform their intended actions.
- Add validation for required fields and invalid input.
- Display loading, success, empty, and error states where appropriate.
- Avoid broken links, dead buttons, placeholder interactions, and console errors.
- Ensure navigation and forms work on desktop and mobile.
- Use appropriate icons and attractive illustrations.
- Add sensible empty states when no donations, requests, or opportunities exist.
- Do not use real personal information in demo data.
- Do not claim any third-party integration is connected unless it has actually been configured.
- Keep the website easy for a student to demonstrate during a college mini-project presentation.

## 17. Testing and Completion

Before considering the website complete:

